WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.500
Cells send newly made proteins to their surface or release them outside.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.360
Think of digestive enzymes and hormones.

3
00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:15.320
The conventional route starts in the rough endoplasmic reticulum, or ER.

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.320
Cargo travels through the Golgi, where it is processed and sorted for delivery.

5
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.860
This pathway connects protein production to release, either straight away or after

6
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.020
storage in a granule.

7
00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:29.303
How do we know the order?

8
00:00:30.143 --> 00:00:34.783
A pulse-chase labels newly made protein briefly, then supplies unlabelled amino

9
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:40.703
acid. In guinea-pig pancreas, that labelled cohort moved from rough ER to Golgi to

10
00:00:40.703 --> 00:00:44.543
granules. The experiment establishes the order of stops.

11
00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.687
Processing starts in the ER lumen.

12
00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:52.827
A core sugar chain is attached, and glucoses are trimmed while the protein folds.

13
00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:57.627
Folded cargo passes through the ERGIC to the cis Golgi.

14
00:00:58.607 --> 00:01:03.527
Golgi enzymes rebuild the chain in order, adding galactose and sialic acid later.

15
00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:06.810
ER residents can escape with cargo.

16
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.610
The KDEL tag is a return-to-sender label.

17
00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:16.570
Its receptor binds escaped residents in the slightly acidic ERGIC and Golgi.

18
00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:22.150
COPI vesicles return them to the ER, where near-neutral conditions let them go.

19
00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.150
In one model, Golgi enzymes also recycle backward.

20
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:32.180
In cisternal maturation, a Golgi sac carries cargo while its resident enzymes

21
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:37.360
change. Enzymes recycle backward as the sac changes from early to late.

22
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.760
In budding yeast, imaging shows this change directly, with cargo staying inside.

23
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:49.140
Maturation is a leading framework, but no single model explains every observation in

24
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.140
the mammalian Golgi.

25
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:53.573
At the trans-Golgi network, the routes split.

26
00:01:54.573 --> 00:01:58.193
Constitutive secretion releases protein as fast as it is made.

27
00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:01.493
Nearly every eukaryotic cell has this route.

28
00:02:02.513 --> 00:02:07.093
Specialized cells also store cargo in granules until a stimulus triggers release.

29
00:02:07.933 --> 00:02:09.493
That is regulated secretion.

30
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:13.093
Sorting can continue inside immature granules.

31
00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.973
A smaller point: Endo H reads part of this processing history.

32
00:02:18.773 --> 00:02:22.733
It cuts high-mannose and hybrid sugar chains, but not complex ones.

33
00:02:24.093 --> 00:02:27.233
Resistance records passage beyond particular Golgi enzymes.

34
00:02:28.193 --> 00:02:30.253
It does not tell you where the protein is now.
